from pathlib import Path
from markdown_it import MarkdownIt
import json,re
root=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
sources=json.loads((root/'sources.js').read_text(encoding='utf-8').split('=',1)[1].rstrip(';'))
md=MarkdownIt('commonmark',{'html':True}).enable('table')
chapters=[];toc=[];stats={'chapters':0,'sections':0,'words':0,'derivation_blocks':0}
for n in range(1,11):
 s=next(s for s in sources if s['module']==n and s['variant']=='main');text=(root/'notes'/f'{n:02}.txt').read_text(encoding='utf-8')
 math=[]
 # Protect TeX from Markdown's escape processing.
 def protect(m):math.append(m[1]);return f'\n\nMATHPLACEHOLDER{len(math)-1}\n\n'
 rendered=md.render(re.sub(r'\$\$(.*?)\$\$',protect,text,flags=re.S))
 from html import escape
 for i,tex in enumerate(math):rendered=rendered.replace(f'<p>MATHPLACEHOLDER{i}</p>',f'<div class="math">$${escape(tex)}$$</div>')
 def head(m):
  nonlocal_id[0]+=1
  return f'<h3 id="m{n}-s{nonlocal_id[0]}">{m[1]}</h3>'
 nonlocal_id=[0];rendered=re.sub(r'<h3>(.*?)</h3>',head,rendered)
 rendered=re.sub(r'<table>(.*?)</table>',rf'<div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Module {n} comparison"><table>\1</table></div>',rendered,flags=re.S)
 page={1:22,2:39,3:23,4:18,5:38,6:21,7:75,8:11,9:31,10:26}[n]
 image=f'<figure><a href="slides/{s["id"]}/{page:03}.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="slides/{s["id"]}/{page:03}.webp" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" alt="Original module {n}, PDF page {page}: supporting diagram and source notation"></a><figcaption>Original M{n}, PDF p. {page}. Open the figure at full size. The surrounding notes explain the assumptions and corrections.</figcaption></figure>'
 links=' · '.join(f'<a href="modules/{__import__("urllib.parse",fromlist=["quote"]).quote(x["file"])}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">{escape(x["file"])} ({len(x["pages"])} pages)</a>' for x in sources if x['module']==n)
 toc.append(f'<a href="#module-{n}"><b>{n:02}</b> {s["title"]}</a>')
 chapters.append(f'<article class="chapter" id="module-{n}"><span class="eyebrow">MODULE {n:02} / PHYSICS 2</span>{rendered}{image}<div class="source-links"><strong>Original files for this chapter</strong><p>{links}</p><a href="index.html#slides/{s["id"]}/1">Read every original page →</a></div><a class="back" href="#contents">↑ Contents</a></article>')
 stats['chapters']+=1;stats['sections']+=nonlocal_id[0];stats['words']+=len(text.split());stats['derivation_blocks']+=len(math)
nav=''.join(toc)
html='''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><link rel="icon" href="assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><meta name="theme-color" content="#def1eb"><title>Physics Reviewer Notes · Field Notes</title><script src="appearance.js"></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="palette.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="assets/katex/katex.min.css"><script src="assets/katex/katex.min.js" defer></script><script src="assets/katex/auto-render.min.js" defer></script><script src="notes-math.js" defer></script><script src="themes.js" defer></script></head><body><a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to notes</a><header class="site-header"><a class="wordmark" href="index.html"><span>∇</span> field notes<small>PHYSICS 2</small></a><nav aria-label="Website sections"><a href="index.html">Home</a><a class="active" href="reviewer.html">Reviewer Notes</a><a href="index.html#practice">Practice</a><a href="index.html#slides">Originals</a></nav><button id="appearance-open" aria-label="Choose a theme">◐</button></header><div class="notes-layout"><aside class="toc"><span class="eyebrow">IN YOUR NOTEBOOK</span>'''+nav+'''<a href="#source-notes">Scope &amp; corrections</a></aside><main id="main" class="reading"><header class="notes-intro" id="contents"><span class="eyebrow">READ · DERIVE · SUBSTITUTE · CHECK</span><h1>Make the<br><em>physics</em> make sense.</h1><p class="lead">A detailed notebook for all ten modules.</p><p>Start with the physical law. Check its assumptions. Trace the derivation, then solve the example with units and directions.</p><div class="callout"><strong>What this reviewer covers</strong><p>All 18 supplied PDFs remain available, including the scanned companion sets. The notes cover their topics and add labeled standard extensions. They do not claim every imaginable exam scenario. Original source errors remain visible in the files; the notes explain corrections.</p></div><details class="mobile-toc"><summary>Jump to a module</summary>'''+nav+'''</details></header>'''+''.join(chapters)+'''<section class="chapter" id="source-notes"><h2>Scope, constants, and source corrections</h2><p>Use each question's supplied constants before the notebook defaults. Common defaults are g = 9.81 m/s², k = 8.99 × 10⁹ N·m²/C², ε₀ = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² F/m, and μ₀ ≈ 4π × 10⁻⁷ T·m/A. Rounded classroom constants can change the final digits.</p><p>Notation matters: ρ can mean mass density, resistivity, or a charge-density symbol depending on the section. C can mean capacitance or heat capacity. E can mean electric field or an explicitly labeled energy. Never substitute by matching a letter alone.</p><h3>Corrections retained beside the calculations</h3><ul><li>M1: density uses kg/m³, not Pa; distinguish absolute and gauge pressures.</li><li>M2: heat is transferred energy, not stored internal energy. Vaporization is not melting. Water does not always expand on heating.</li><li>M3: use consistent component directions in the rectangle example. Neutrino oscillations show that neutrinos are not all massless.</li><li>M5: field work is negative potential-energy change; voltage is energy per charge.</li><li>M7: preserve unrounded simultaneous equations and distinguish resistance from resistivity.</li><li>M8: distinguish wire length from loop area and the area-normal angle from the plane angle.</li><li>M9: a generator converts mechanical energy to electrical energy. A transformer alone does not make DC. Lenz's law opposes flux change.</li><li>M10: photoelectron maximum kinetic energy depends on frequency. The water–glass–air exit angle is about 26.40°, not 20° for the stated indices and parallel faces.</li></ul><p>The supplied PDFs are the primary course sources. Supplementary checks: <a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/13-2-lenzs-law">Lenz's law</a> and <a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/6-2-photoelectric-effect">photoelectric effect</a>. Explanations and worked calculations here are independently written. Scanned companion screenshots contain embedded pages; their PDF page numbers differ from the embedded slide numbers.</p><p>Use your browser's Find to locate a term. Use Print to save the notes as a PDF. All chapters remain open for reading.</p></section></main></div><footer>Field Notes · Your course, worked through.</footer></body></html>'''
(root/'reviewer.html').write_text(html,encoding='utf-8');(root/'note-stats.json').write_text(json.dumps(stats),encoding='utf-8');print(stats)
